## Lore

The Corrupt are among the most despicable servants of evil in the world. They
were once humans (sometimes D-bees) who willingly gave up their humanity,
perhaps their souls, to a nameless, faceless power they call the Liberator -
some suggest one of the Palladium World''s Old Ones. Unlike Witches they pray to
and follow no master. Their one command is to go forth and reap sorrow, death
and havoc; they gather in small cults or serve Lord Dunscon, evil dragons,
sorcerers and dark gods for a while, but never for long.

The transformation remakes the body into something larger and monstrous: dark
scales, glowing orb eyes, wicked claws and living, spiked black armor. They
feed on the P.P.E. released at death, and only by killing. They heal at a
phenomenal rate, do not sicken and may not age, but once slain they cannot
return.

The mind grows less intelligent and more savage, predatory and cunning. Love,
joy and tenderness are gone; only hate, anger and the joy of inflicting pain
remain. In combat many succumb to a euphoric bloodlust that can end in days of
torture and hundreds dead. They respect power, but accept no being as master,
not even the gods; a Corrupt in service grows agitated and disobedient, then
one day is simply gone - or turns on his allies when the battle turns.

## The Elite (Hidden) Corrupt

Those who were evil to begin with and had strong will (M.E. 15 or higher) may
keep their normal outward appearance, rotten inside like a beautiful apple.
They are just as vile, but hard to identify, and serve as spies or lone agents.
They have half the usual M.D.C. (P.E. x5 halved, +1D6 per level) and lose only
10% of I.Q. and M.A. When angry or distressed their body shows scales and their
eyes glow dimly.

## Villain and Player Character

The book is plain that the transformation is so dramatic that the Corrupt is
more of an R.C.C. than an O.C.C.: the character becomes a monster, and it is
printed as an NPC villain. If a G.M. allows it as a player character, it can
only be the evil monster, or the super-rare Repentant.

## The Repentant (Cursed) Corrupt

Sometimes a part of the person hates what he has done and never gives his
whole self to the benefactor. The Repentant has the full appearance and most of
the powers of the monster (never an Elite), but keeps most of his memories,
alignment and identity. There is no known way back. Because he did not fully
commit:

- M.D.C. is P.E. x4, +1D6 per level.
- He can cast only two of the spells (player''s choice).
- He remembers half his old skills (player''s choice), frozen forever at their
  proficiency at the moment of transformation, and gets half the new skill
  selections.
- Combat bonuses are halved.
- If his alignment is unprincipled or good, demon slaying and holy weapons do
  only normal damage to him.

## Equipment and Money

Little need for equipment beyond weapons and perhaps more body armor; drawn to
magic weapons and items, Vibro-Blades, and plasma and particle beam weapons.
Typically starts with two ancient style weapons and two modern energy weapons.

Money: 2D6x100 credits'' worth of gold, silver or gems and 1D6x100 in universal
credits. They hoard stolen valuables rather than spend them.

## GM Notes

Average level of experience 3-6; they are slothful, and 70% never pass 6th
level. The Corrupt are found only in North America, most in the Magic Zone and
the East. They see Cyber-Knights, Mystic Knights, Battle Magi, Grim Reapers and
Psi-Stalkers as natural enemies and deadly rivals.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'corrupt');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'corrupt';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-corrupt-class.sql');
